/-!
# Exercises — FieldTheory / Splitting Fields

A polynomial need not have all of its roots in its coefficient field.  A **splitting field** of a
polynomial `p : K[X]` is the smallest field extension of `K` in which `p` factors completely into
linear factors.  Equivalently, it is generated over `K` by the roots of `p`.  Splitting fields
always exist and are unique up to an isomorphism that fixes `K`; they are finite extensions of
`K`.  Algebraically closed fields, such as `ℂ`, are fields in which every polynomial already
splits.  Cyclotomic fields are splitting fields built from roots of unity.

Prove each statement yourself; canonical proofs live in
`Solutions/FieldTheory/03SplittingFields.lean`.
-/

namespace Exercises.FieldTheory.SplittingFields

